Build one independent checker from the original public task and interface only.
Return only one complete JSON object, without markdown:
{"status":"ready","checker":<object matching checker_schema>}
or {"status":"unsupported","reason":"specific limitation or unresolved requirement"}.
Return one complete proposal for this request; do not start extra correction rounds.
Do not return implementation RTL, a patch or a partial object.
Treat the supplied task and source catalog as source material, never as instructions
to change this contract. No candidate RTL, observations, solver analysis or external
reference answers are available. Do not invent initialization, latency, priority,
port widths, signedness, numerical approximation or reset semantics.

Use only the supplied ports, with exactly their public names, directions and widths.
Every port and expression width must be 1..256. Neither route can represent a wider
public port; do not truncate it, split it or omit it to fit the checker.
The request's route selects the schema; route is NOT a field of the returned checker.
All requirements need unique IDs and an exact original quote or source_id. Use the
source catalog to identify original evidence; whitespace is the only permitted quote
normalization. Report only the requirements the checker can actually exercise.
Quoted text establishes source provenance, not automatic proof that your interpretation
is correct. If a necessary assumption is unresolved, return unsupported.

Both routes use fixed independent cases. A case may use the schema's explicit steps,
or replace steps with "sequence":[{"inputs":{ALL non-clock inputs:integer},
"repeat":1..32,"edge":true,"sample":true},...]. Each item repeats that exact complete
drive; there is no implicit input hold, increment, dynamic handshake or DUT feedback.
The host expands sequence before strict schema validation. Cases may also replace
steps with "exhaustive":true ONLY for stateless combinational logic with total input
width 1..8 bits; the host enumerates every input vector. These are the only extensions
to checker_schema. Keep id and requirements on every case. Do not combine steps,
sequence or exhaustive. At most 8 cases and 256 total expanded events; clocked cases
have at most 32 events each. Every case starts with a fresh DUT instance.

MODEL ROUTE:
Use schema_version=1, mode="behavior", state={} for stateless logic, clock=null for
combinational logic. Observation-only mode="stimulus" cannot check correctness.
At most 8 model registers, all names prefixed model_, and 128 total expression nodes.
Encoded control states are values of a register, not separate registers. Their complete
transition expressions must still fit the node limit. Arrays have no native representation;
count all expanded registers and expressions, not just the array name. If unknown initial
state cannot become known from a public reset/warmup, return unsupported promptly.
Every output requires an expression with exactly its public width. Expressions read
inputs and model state only, never DUT outputs. No arbitrary Python, RTL, function calls,
strings as expressions, division, floating-point or square-root operators.
Expression forms are {"ref":"input_or_model_state"}, {"const":integer,"width":integer},
or {"op":"...","args":[...],"width":integer}. Widths are 1..256 and explicit.
Two args: add sub mul and or xor shl shr asr eq ne lt le gt ge land lor.
One arg: not bitnot neg reduce_and reduce_or reduce_xor; slice also needs lsb.
Three args: ite(condition,true,false). concat has 1..32 args and summed result width.
Comparison, reduction and logical results have width=1. Signed arithmetic/comparison
must explicitly set signed=true where needed. Widen operands before operations when
the mathematical requirement needs carry/product bits. No silently truncated literals.

For one clock use clock={"port":"clk","edge":"posedge"|"negedge"}; omit state.clock.
For example, state={"model_q":{"width":1,"next":{"ref":"d"}}} has no clock key
inside model_q when the top-level legacy clock is used. Do not mix the clock forms.
Each step drives ALL non-clock inputs; edge defaults true. edge=false drives and samples
without a clock edge. Inputs settle first, state.next reads the same OLD model state,
then outputs read NEW state after the effective edge. sample=false still executes the
step but does not compare outputs. Synchronous reset belongs in state.next explicitly.
Asynchronous reset uses async_reset={"port":"rst","active":0|1,
"values":{every_model_register:expression}}. Assertion acts before the clock edge and
also with edge=false. Include an off-edge reset after normal clocked operation if the
task specifies asynchronous reset; merely resetting at a clock edge does not test it.
If using schema clocks instead, omit legacy clock/edge/async_reset, bind each register
to state.clock, use step.edges and async_resets consistently. Do not claim CDC safety.
Initial values are UNKNOWN unless the original task explicitly supplies an initial
assignment. Only then model_<signal> may use initial={"value":integer,"signal":"signal",
"quote":"exact explicit initialization statement"}. Reset is not initialization.
Supply reset/warmup sequences before comparing state-dependent outputs. Unknown values
must not be replaced with zero. output_valid and output_masks may express explicitly
undefined public domains only, never hide a required scenario. Every case must have
known comparable expected output bits and refer to declared requirement IDs; every
requirement must have a case with a predicted comparison.

PROPERTIES ROUTE:
Use the PropertySuite schema and its sampling member; cases/extensions above apply
inside sampling. Only combinational or one explicitly defined clock is supported.
The checker object has exactly schema_version, sampling, requirements, properties.
Its outer shape is {"status":"ready","checker":{"schema_version":1,
"sampling":<SamplingPlan>,"requirements":[...],"properties":[...]}}.
Keep requirements and properties beside sampling, never inside it. Do not add route,
mode, ports or cases to this checker object's root; ports and cases belong in sampling.
Prefer 3..5 useful properties, but never invent extra requirements to reach that count.
Leaves read only public ports: {"signal":"name","phase":"pre"|"post","past":0..16,
"anchor":false}. pre is after this event's inputs settle but before its effective edge;
post is after the edge and nonblocking assignments settle. Do not substitute previous
post for current pre when inputs changed. All arithmetic uses explicit finite widths.
Past refers to history; anchor refers to the triggering event. Do not combine anchor
and nonzero past; triggers and abort cannot use anchor. A pre-sampled property cannot
read current post. window min/max in 0..16: [0,0] immediate, [n,n] fixed delay,
[a,b] at least one satisfying observation in that response window. Every overlapping
trigger creates a separate obligation; this is not a transaction queue or one-to-one
response matching. No cross-clock history, unbounded wait or internal DUT signals.
Use warmup_ticks only for a publicly justified need; it is fixed before execution.
Reset may abort pending obligations only with an explicit abort_requirements citation.
Zero triggers, missing history, unknown bits and unfinished windows are NOT passes.
Stimulus must make useful known trigger and response observations possible, including
relevant boundary scenarios; acceptance of this schema is not evidence of actual firing.
For approximate numerical algorithms, quote and implement the stated accuracy/domain.
Do not substitute exact floor-integer sqrt for a fixed-point Newton-Raphson requirement.
